#Convert the variable educ below into a factor variable with each value coded explicitly as one of the education levels.
#Tabulate the variable using table() function.
#Change the order of the levels of this factor variable such that the most common education level is the first (baseline/reference) category (you can leave other education levels in their original order).
#Tabulate the variable again.
set.seed(2025)
educ <- rpois(1000, 2)
educ <- ifelse(educ > 4, 4, educ)
# Exercise 3: Vectorized Operations
# Assuming that valid IDs consist of 2 random uppercase letters followed by a random integer between 4 and 6 digits (e.g. AB1234, ZQ567890), check the vector of IDs below and determine what proportion of values are valid.
# You don’t need to create loops to solve this exercise.
#Use vectorized operations instead.
#Report either the proportion or percentage of valid IDs in the vector.
# Tip: You can use grepl() function to check if a string in character vector matches certain conditions.
#See further details on regular expressions in R for more information on specifying different conditions (aka creating matching patterns).
# Another useful function that can come in handy is nchar(), which allows you to determine the number of characters in a string
set.seed(2025)
ids <- mapply(function(x) paste0(
paste0(sample(LETTERS, sample.int(3, 1)), collapse = ""), sample.int(100000, 1),
collapse = ""), 1:1000
)
